# Code Reusability Guidelines

## DRY vs YAGNI Balance

### When to Extract Code

- Code is duplicated in 2+ places
- Logic is complex and benefits from abstraction
- Code will likely be reused in the future
- Abstraction improves readability

### When NOT to Extract

- Code is only used once (YAGNI - You Aren't Gonna Need It)
- Premature abstraction before understanding use cases
- Over-abstraction that makes code harder to understand
- Simple code that's clear as-is

## Shared Layer Guidelines

### What Belongs in Shared

- UI components used across multiple features
- Utility functions (formatDate, validateEmail, etc.)
- Constants and configuration
- Types that are used everywhere
- Hooks that are truly generic

### Shared Structure

```
shared/
├── ui/              # Reusable UI components
├── lib/             # Utility functions
├── hooks/           # Generic hooks
├── constants/       # App-wide constants
└── types/           # Shared types
```

## Entity vs Feature vs Shared

### Entity Layer

- Business entities (Event, Task, User)
- Entity-specific UI components
- Entity-specific hooks
- Entity-specific API functions

### Feature Layer

- Business logic and features
- Feature-specific UI
- Feature-specific hooks
- Feature-specific state

### Shared Layer

- Generic utilities
- Common UI components (Button, Input, Modal)
- Generic hooks (useToggle, useDebounce)
- App-wide constants

## Extracting Reusable Code

### When to Extract to Hook

```typescript
// ✅ Good - extract to hook when used in multiple components
export function useEventCard(event: Event) {
  const [isExpanded, setIsExpanded] = useState(false);
  const updateMutation = useUpdateEvent();

  const handleToggle = () => setIsExpanded(!isExpanded);
  const handleEdit = () => {/* ... */};

  return { isExpanded, handleToggle, handleEdit };
}

// ❌ Bad - keep in component if only used once
export function EventCard({ event }: EventCardProps) {
  const [isExpanded, setIsExpanded] = useState(false);
  // ... logic
}
```

### When to Extract to Utility

```typescript
// ✅ Good - extract utility when used multiple times
export function formatEventDate(date: Date): string {
  return dayjs(date).format('DD.MM.YYYY HH:mm');
}

// Usage in multiple components
formatEventDate(event.date);

// ❌ Bad - keep inline if only used once
const formattedDate = dayjs(event.date).format('DD.MM.YYYY HH:mm');
```

### When to Extract to Component

```typescript
// ✅ Good - extract component when reused
export function EventCardHeader({ event }: { event: Event }) {
  return (
    <header>
      <h3>{event.title}</h3>
      <EventMeta event={event} />
    </header>
  );
}

// ❌ Bad - keep inline if only used once
<header>
  <h3>{event.title}</h3>
  <div>{formatDate(event.date)}</div>
</header>
```

## Component Reusability

### Generic Components

```typescript
// ✅ Good - generic, reusable component
interface ButtonProps {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary';
  size?: 'small' | 'medium' | 'large';
  onClick?: () => void;
}

export function Button({ children, variant, size, onClick }: ButtonProps) {
  return (
    <button
      className={clsx(styles.button, styles[variant], styles[size])}
      onClick={onClick}
    >
      {children}
    </button>
  );
}

// ❌ Bad - too specific, not reusable
export function CreateEventButton() {
  return <button className={styles.createEvent}>Create Event</button>;
}
```

### Composable Components

```typescript
// ✅ Good - composable component
interface CardProps {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  header?: React.ReactNode;
  footer?: React.ReactNode;
}

export function Card({ children, header, footer }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <div className={styles.card}>
      {header && <header>{header}</header>}
      <div className={styles.content}>{children}</div>
      {footer && <footer>{footer}</footer>}
    </div>
  );
}

// ❌ Bad - rigid, not composable
export function EventCard({ event }: { event: Event }) {
  return (
    <div className={styles.card}>
      <header>{event.title}</header>
      <div>{event.content}</div>
      <footer>
        <button>Edit</button>
      </footer>
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Hook Reusability

### Generic Hooks

```typescript
// ✅ Good - generic hook
export function useToggle(initialValue = false) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState(initialValue);
  const toggle = () => setValue((prev) => !prev);
  return [value, toggle] as const;
}

// ❌ Bad - too specific
export function useEventModal() {
  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);
  const toggle = () => setIsOpen((prev) => !prev);
  return [isOpen, toggle] as const;
}
```

### Specialized Hooks

```typescript
// ✅ Good - specialized hook using generic hooks
export function useEventModal() {
  const [isOpen, toggle] = useToggle();
  const [selectedEvent, setSelectedEvent] = useState<Event | null>(null);

  const open = (event: Event) => {
    setSelectedEvent(event);
    toggle();
  };

  const close = () => {
    setSelectedEvent(null);
    toggle();
  };

  return { isOpen, selectedEvent, open, close };
}
```

## Utility Function Guidelines

### Pure Functions

```typescript
// ✅ Good - pure function, easily testable
export function formatDate(date: Date, format = 'DD.MM.YYYY'): string {
  return dayjs(date).format(format);
}

// ❌ Bad - side effects, not reusable
export function formatEventDate(event: Event): string {
  console.log('Formatting date for event:', event.id);
  return dayjs(event.date).format('DD.MM.YYYY');
}
```

### Single Responsibility

```typescript
// ✅ Good - single responsibility
export function validateEmail(email: string): boolean {
  return /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(email);
}

export function validateUrl(url: string): boolean {
  try {
    new URL(url);
    return true;
  } catch {
    return false;
  }
}

// ❌ Bad - multiple responsibilities
export function validateInput(input: string, type: 'email' | 'url'): boolean {
  if (type === 'email') {
    return /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(input);
  }
  try {
    new URL(input);
    return true;
  } catch {
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Avoiding Over-Abstraction

### When Abstraction is Too Much

```typescript
// ❌ Bad - over-abstraction
export function createHandler<T extends string>(
  type: T,
  callback: (event: Event) => void
) {
  return function handler(event: Event) {
    if (event.type === type) {
      callback(event);
    }
  };
}

// ✅ Good - simple and clear
export function handleEventClick(event: Event) {
  console.log('Event clicked:', event.id);
}

export function handleEventDelete(event: Event) {
  deleteEvent(event.id);
}
```

## Code Reusability Checklist

- [ ] Code is duplicated in 2+ places before extracting
- [ ] Abstraction improves readability
- [ ] Shared code is truly generic
- [ ] Components are composable
- [ ] Hooks are reusable
- [ ] Utilities are pure functions
- [ ] Code follows single responsibility
- [ ] Avoided premature abstraction
- [ ] Code is testable
- [ ] Documentation is clear
